{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d210ef559bb744599ec946176abf9f15","ordinal":601,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.101","text":"\n\nNor will each be a part of itself, for because of the containing, something will be greater and smaller than itself. If, then, the things said to be parts are parts neither of the whole, nor of themselves, nor of one another, they are parts of nothing. And if they are parts of nothing, there are no parts, for relatives are abolished along with one another. Let this much be said in passing, as a digression, since we have happened to mention whole and part."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-becf2eb840d34a7699077005a2218572","ordinal":602,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.102","text":"\n\nSome say that what is called natural change has no reality either, and they try arguments like these. If something changes, what changes is either a body or something incorporeal. But each of these has been called into question, so the account of change will be puzzling too."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6c548e3934184b5e84e3f3c0b32ec890","ordinal":603,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.103","text":"\n\nIf something changes, it changes by being acted upon, through certain activities of a cause. ⟨.....⟩ For the reality of the cause is overturned, and the thing acted upon is overturned along with it, since it has nothing to be acted upon by. So nothing changes either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1790c83ed7084a80b58e25148cd8a0fc","ordinal":604,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.104","text":"\n\nIf something changes, either what is changes or what is not. Now what is not has no reality and can neither be acted upon nor act, so it does not admit of change either. And if what is changes, it changes either insofar as it is, or insofar as it is not."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-18d5ab6295be4d7db40303cc9ed15e3d","ordinal":605,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.105","text":"\n\nInsofar as it is not, it does not change, for it is not something that is not. And if it changes insofar as it is, it will be other than being, that is, it will be what is not. But it is absurd to say that what is becomes what is not, so what is does not change either. And if neither what is changes nor what is not, and there is nothing besides these, it remains to say that nothing changes. Some also say the following."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2a025722538242b6a5c243405ca5f007","ordinal":606,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.106","text":"\n\nWhat changes must change in some time. But nothing changes in past time or in future time, nor yet in present time, as we shall show. So nothing changes. Now nothing changes in past or future time, since neither of these is present, and it is impossible to act or be acted upon in a time that does not exist and is not present. Nor does anything change in present time."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-48bffb7898e14bfaa5d35529f869784d","ordinal":607,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.107","text":"\n\nFor present time is perhaps non-existent as well. But to set that aside for now, it has no parts, and it is impossible to suppose that in a partless time iron, for example, changes from hard to soft, or that any of the other changes takes place, since these appear to need duration. If, then, nothing changes in past time, nor in future time, ⟨nor in present time, then we should not⟩ say that anything changes."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-20cfaba1def3446eaf802fdf284c7124","ordinal":608,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.108","text":"\n\nFurthermore, if there is such a thing as change, either ⟨......⟩ the senses are affected in a simple way, whereas change seems to involve remembering together both what something changes from and what it is said to change into. And if it is an object of thought, then, since there has been undecidable disagreement among the ancients about the existence of objects of thought, as we have often pointed out already, we shall have nothing to say about the existence of change either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8d2737982a0a4e94b5ab5a70f20601ef","ordinal":609,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.109","text":"\n\nComing into being and perishing are overturned along with addition, subtraction and natural change, since without these nothing would come into being or perish. For instance, when the ten perishes, as they say, a nine comes into being through the subtraction of a unit; the ten comes into being when the nine perishes, through the addition of a unit; and rust comes into being when bronze perishes, through change. So if the motions just mentioned are abolished, coming into being and perishing must perhaps be abolished too. Nonetheless, some also say the following."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-710e120dad7c4889895b4ed094992d7f","ordinal":610,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.110","text":"\n\nIf Socrates was born, ⟨either⟩ Socrates came into being when Socrates was not, or when Socrates already was. But if he were said to have been born when he already was, he would have been born twice. And if he was born when he was not, Socrates both was and was not at the same time: he was, by having come into being, and he was not, by hypothesis."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-01443031e85949c68f601737363982aa","ordinal":611,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.111","text":"\n\nAnd if Socrates died, he died either while he was alive or after he had died. He did not die while he was alive, since then the same man would be both alive and dead. Nor did he die after he had died, since then he would have died twice. So Socrates did not die. By applying this argument to each of the things said to come into being or perish, one can abolish coming into being and perishing."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b2a005e808ed4f349d046ffff649a862","ordinal":612,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.112","text":"\n\nSome also put the argument this way. If something comes into being, either what is comes into being or what is not. What is not does not come into being, since nothing can happen to what is not, and so neither can coming into being. Nor does what is. For if what is comes into being, it comes into being either insofar as it is or insofar as it is not. Insofar as it is not, it does not come into being. And if it comes into being insofar as it is, then, since they say that what comes into being comes into being as one thing out of another, what comes into being will be other than what is, that is, it will be what is not. So what comes into being will be what is not, which is incoherent."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5c74cabd8c2949a19c9aeccb18472a8a","ordinal":613,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.113","text":"\n\nIf, then, neither what is not nor what is comes into being, nothing comes into being. By the same reasoning nothing perishes either. For if something perishes, either what is perishes or what is not. What is not does not perish, since what perishes must be acted upon. Nor does what is. For it perishes either while remaining in being or while not remaining. And if it perishes while remaining in being, the same thing will at once be what is and what is not:"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-55713db48c7546e38c28bdbcb40167cb","ordinal":614,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.114","text":"\n\nfor since it perishes not insofar as it is not but insofar as it is, then insofar as it is said to have perished, it will be other than what is, and therefore what is not; but insofar as it is said to perish while remaining in being, it will be what is. But it is absurd to say that the same thing both is and is not, so what is does not perish while remaining in being. And suppose what is perishes not while remaining in being, but first passes over into not being and only then perishes. Then it is no longer what is that perishes but what is not, and we have pointed out that this is impossible. If, then, neither what is nor what is not perishes, and there is nothing besides these, nothing perishes. This much, said as in an outline, will be enough about motions too. From it follows that the dogmatists’ study of nature has no existence and cannot be conceived."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-29594222a62a4208b28f98b8d95bc7d1","ordinal":615,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.115","text":"\n\nFollowing on from this, some have also raised puzzles about rest as it occurs in nature. They say that what is in motion is not at rest, and that on the dogmatists’ assumptions every body is continually in motion. For the dogmatists say that substance is in flux and is always giving off effluences and receiving additions. That is why Plato says bodies are not even things that are, and prefers to call them things that come to be, and why Heraclitus likens the ready mobility of our matter to the swift current of a river. So no body is at rest."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0a2ca0f4a8164e56ab1de23974fdb79a","ordinal":616,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.116","text":"\n\nMoreover, what is said to be at rest seems to be held together by what surrounds it, and what is held together is acted upon. But nothing is acted upon, since there is no cause either, as we pointed out. So nothing is at rest. Some also put forward this argument: what is at rest is acted upon, and what is acted upon is moved; so what is said to be at rest is moved; and if it is moved, it is not at rest."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-32e52d7c9f2f4d68bf626254e81f0cad","ordinal":617,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.117","text":"\n\nFrom this it is clear that nothing incorporeal can be at rest either. For if what is at rest is acted upon, and being acted upon is peculiar to bodies, if to anything, and not to incorporeals, then nothing incorporeal can either be acted upon or be at rest. So nothing is at rest."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-67dfd42d559f4da8a5e5ea30378f8171","ordinal":618,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.118","text":"\n\nLet this much be said about rest too. But since none of the things discussed above is conceived without place or time, we must move on to examine these. For if one shows that these have no reality, each of those will have no reality on their account too. Let us begin with place."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-aeadea0f507747e0aff47eb9727c6d4d","ordinal":619,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.119","text":"\n\nPlace is spoken of in two ways, strictly and loosely. Loosely, [as] place in the broad sense, as the city is my place; strictly, the place that occupies something precisely, by which I am precisely contained. Our inquiry, then, is about ⟨the⟩ place in the precise sense. Some have posited it, some have abolished it, and some have suspended judgment about it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3b26470f0b694aca9eecbe24fe93a6f8","ordinal":620,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.120","text":"\n\nOf these, those who say that it exists take refuge in clarity. For who, they say, would claim that there is no place when he sees the parts of place, such as right and left, up and down, front and back; when he is now here and now somewhere else; when he sees that where my teacher used to hold his discussions, I am holding mine now; when he apprehends that things light by nature have a different place from things heavy by nature;"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b1b261b1af6d472c833418622029ea02","ordinal":621,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.121","text":"\n\nand when, besides, he hears the ancients saying, “First of all Chaos came to be”? For they say that Chaos is place, from its being able to make room for the things that come to be in it. Moreover, if there is any body, they say, there is also place, for without place there would be no body. And if there is the “by which” and the “from which”, there is also the “in which”, and that is place. But the first part holds in each of the two cases; so the second part holds in both.\n〔1〕 The line is from Hesiod’s Theogony (116); the Greek links chaos with chōrein, “to make room for”."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f57111be75fd41e1943d339165ba6214","ordinal":622,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.122","text":"\n\nThose who abolish place do not grant that the parts of place exist either. For place, they say, is nothing apart from its parts, and whoever tries to conclude that place exists by taking its parts as existing is trying to establish what is in question by means of itself. Likewise, those who say that something comes to be or has come to be in some place are talking nonsense, when place is not granted at all. They are also helping themselves to the existence of body, which is not granted outright; and the “from which” and the “by which” are shown to be non-existent in the same way as place."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b338f29524c842888651e9df70250677","ordinal":623,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.123","text":"\n\nAnd Hesiod is not a trustworthy judge of philosophical questions. Having fended off in this way the considerations brought forward to establish that there is place, they go on to establish, in more elaborate ways, that it does not exist. They make use of the dogmatists’ positions on place that are thought to carry the most weight, those of the Stoics and the Peripatetics, in the following manner."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6a848f52dba1481887b582fb20685b67","ordinal":624,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.124","text":"\n\nThe Stoics say that void is what can be occupied by an existent but is not occupied, or an interval empty of body, or an interval not occupied by body. Place is an interval occupied by an existent and equal to what occupies it (here they are calling body “an existent”). Room is an interval occupied by body in one part and unoccupied in another. Some, however, have said that room is the place of a large body, so that the difference between place and room lies in size."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-aa92f217c3364e8b93418f6b88960fad","ordinal":625,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.125","text":"\n\nNow it is said in reply: since they say that place is an interval occupied by body, in what sense do they call it an interval? Is it the length of the body, or its breadth, or its depth alone, or the three dimensions? If it is one dimension, the place is not equal to what it is the place of. Besides, the container would then be part of the contained, which is utterly incoherent."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a67b3c9e837e4c30ab41fed1e9f404d1","ordinal":626,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.126","text":"\n\nAnd if it is the three dimensions, then, since neither void nor any other body having dimension underlies what is called place, but only the body said to be in place, which is made up of the dimensions (for it is length, breadth, depth and resistance, and resistance is said to be an accident of the dimensions just mentioned), the body itself will be its own place, and the same thing will be container and contained, which is absurd. So there is no dimension belonging to an underlying place. For this reason place is not anything either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7003ceb68df94fd8a6a4fa47c2712eec","ordinal":627,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.127","text":"\n\nThis argument is also put forward. In each of the things said to be in place we do not observe double dimensions, but one length, one breadth and one depth. Do these dimensions, then, belong to the body alone, or to the place alone, or to both? If they belong to the place alone, the body will have no length, breadth or depth of its own at all, so that the body will not even be a body, which is absurd."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d8a2950e669a4e63b6521c29c9a335df","ordinal":628,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.128","text":"\n\nIf they belong to both, then, since the void has no reality apart from the dimensions, if the dimensions of the void underlie the body and constitute the body itself, what constitutes the void will also constitute the body. For about the existence of resistance it is not possible to make firm assertions, as we pointed out earlier. And since only the dimensions appear in what is called body, and these belong to the void and are the same as the void, the body will be void, which is absurd. And if the dimensions belong to the body alone, there will be no dimension of place, and therefore no place either. If, then, no dimension of place is found in any of the ways just described, place does not exist."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3ef5c28588264a03b495e9471c8b49aa","ordinal":629,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.129","text":"\n\nIn addition, it is said that when body enters the void and place comes about, the void either stays, or withdraws, or perishes. If it stays, the same thing will be both full and void. If it withdraws, moving from one place to another, or perishes by changing, the void will be a body, since these are affections peculiar to body. But it is absurd to say that the same thing is void and full, or that the void is a body. So it is absurd to say that the void can be occupied by body and become place."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1559a1f1af4b4db2ad3786b34a8aeedf","ordinal":630,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.130","text":"\n\nFor these reasons the void too is found to have no reality, if it cannot be occupied by body and become place; for void was said to be what can be occupied by body. Room is overturned along with these. If room is the large place, it is canceled along with place; if it is the interval occupied by body in one part and empty in another, it is abolished along with both."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-cb992e8ef63b48d391cbe6e123046b6a","ordinal":631,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.131","text":"\n\nThese things, and more besides, are said against the Stoics’ position on place. The Peripatetics say that place is the limit of the container insofar as it contains, so that my place is the surface of the air that is molded around my body. But if this is place, the same thing will both be and not be. When a body is about to come to be in some place, then, insofar as nothing can come to be in what does not exist, the place must exist beforehand, so that the body may come to be in it; and for this reason the place will exist before the body that is in the place comes to be in it. But insofar as place is brought about when the surface of the container is molded around the contained, place cannot exist before the body comes to be in it; and for this reason it will not exist then. But it is absurd to say that the same thing both is and is not. So place is not the limit of the container insofar as it contains."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fbf41bd6e9ad499db56b78f1ede465db","ordinal":632,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.132","text":"\n\nFurther, if place is something, it is either generated or ungenerated. It is not ungenerated, since it is brought about, they say, by being molded around the body in it. Nor is it generated. For if it is generated, the place comes into being either when the body is in place (the place in which what is in place is already said to be), or when the body is not in it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-279c1c48dcf849ce8f20d61bcde4d540","ordinal":633,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.133","text":"\n\nBut it does not come into being when the body is in it, since the place already belongs to the body in it. Nor does it come into being when the body is not in it, if, as they say, the container is molded around the contained and so becomes place, and nothing can be molded around what is not in it. And if place comes into being neither when the body is in place nor when it is not in it, and nothing besides these can be conceived, then place is not generated. And if it is neither generated nor ungenerated, it does not exist."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c5bf4ab343b14995af7e3187c9ce2446","ordinal":634,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.134","text":"\n\nThese points can also be made more generally. If place is something, it is either body or incorporeal; but each of these is puzzling, as we pointed out; so place is puzzling too. Place is conceived in relation to the body it is the place of; but the account of the existence of body is puzzling; so the account of place is puzzling too. The place of each thing is not eternal; but if it is said to come into being, it is found to have no reality, since there is no such thing as coming into being."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3be1b144fc5440e9a0db0ac313afa062","ordinal":635,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.135","text":"\n\nMany other things could be said. But so as not to drag out the discussion, we must add this: the skeptics are given pause by the arguments, but they are also abashed by the clarity. So we attach ourselves to neither side, as far as what the dogmatists say goes, but suspend judgment about place."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-00350dcec0c64d10b9b069543adb68ad","ordinal":636,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.136","text":"\n\nThe same thing happens to us in the inquiry into time. As far as appearances go, time seems to be something; but as far as what is said about it goes, it appears to have no reality. For some say that time is the interval of the motion of the whole (by “the whole” I mean the cosmos), others that it is the motion of the cosmos itself. Aristotle (or, as some say, Plato) says it is the number of the before and after in motion. Strato (or, as some say, Aristotle) says it is"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-cc70aec80aa74af98f565c2c397f033f","ordinal":637,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.137","text":"\n\nthe measure of motion and rest. Epicurus, as Demetrius the Laconian reports, says it is an accident of accidents, attending on days and nights, on seasons, on feelings and absences of feeling, on motions and rests.\n〔1〕 Demetrius of Laconia was an Epicurean philosopher of the second century BC."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4766c6e79da14755bc238b2492a5715b","ordinal":638,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.138","text":"\n\nAs to its substance, some have said that it is a body, like Aenesidemus and his followers (for they say it does not differ from what is and from the primary body), and others that it is incorporeal. Either, then, all these positions are true, or all are false, or some are true and some false. They cannot all be true, since most of them conflict; nor will the dogmatists grant that they are all false."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9a4dd400360b463cb1fcfee86e0f91f2","ordinal":639,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.139","text":"\n\nBesides, if it were granted that it is false that time is a body, and false too that it is incorporeal, the non-existence of time would be granted outright, since there can be nothing besides these. Nor is it possible to apprehend which are true and which false, because of the equipollent disagreement and the impasse about the criterion and proof. So for these reasons we shall be unable to affirm anything firmly about time."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a0dd04db083c42dc9483e7adcab17a6f","ordinal":640,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.140","text":"\n\nThen again, time does not seem to exist without motion, or rest too. So when motion is abolished, and rest likewise, time is abolished. Nonetheless, some also say the following against time. If there is ⟨any⟩"}]}